{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794830381958,"sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","part":{"id":"prt_1e43a3386001DQYas1G7JVSi7G","sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","messageID":"msg_1e43a22eb0018NAF1o5GfH3pdw","type":"step-start","snapshot":"f55bbc389ce46b4baaee1878203194faa50141ac"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794830405358,"sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","part":{"partID":"prt_1e43a8bb9001bODuARP7fZvxPf","sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","messageID":"msg_1e43a22eb0018NAF1o5GfH3pdw","type":"tool","id":"call_3487219b310a493589269fec","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '100,140p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | cut -c1-260"},"output":"- Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current director\n- Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `liveRows`, `totalBytes`, `medianBytes`, `maxBytes`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes`, `headBytes`, `estimatedTokens` and `live`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length\n- Refusal: a run with no live head prints `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}` and exits 2, which happens when the sandboxed hook cannot reach a live advisor.\n- Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. Each hook process also runs without `AI_SESSION_CHILD`, so a run from a dis\n\n### Two arms on one commit\n\n`run-command-behaviour.mjs --arm off` and `--arm on` measure one commit twice. A run under either arm starts from an environment where the advisor switch comes from the arm and not from the operator's shell, so the turn environment differs only in the advisor \n\n`--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_\n\n`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. Before it\n\nA summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` an\n\nEach arm's `startRate`, `startedOfOffered` and each policy's `started` count in `byPolicy` follow the start rule below. `startShapes` prints the lookups of the start rows as `readOnly`, so adding it back restores the `startRate` numerator of the old rule. It d\n\n### Reading the outcome\n\n- A loaded document is not a followed step, so `loadRate` and `followedRange` say a workflow opened, not that its steps were taken.\n- The behaviour rows are tuned rows, so `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` describe the corpus the brief was built against, not a fresh one.\n- Each row ran once, so one run moves a rate by more than the range around it can settle.\n- Two arms differ in more than the brief, so a `--pair` gain or loss is not the brief's effect alone.\n- The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, so the split by offer says what the Claude head carried for that row.\n\n---\n\n## 4. STRUCTURE\n\n| Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n| `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n| `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n| `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false off\n| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one sess\n| `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRun\n| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`\n| `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n| `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps th\n| `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"- Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current director\n- Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `liveRows`, `totalBytes`, `medianBytes`, `maxBytes`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes`, `headBytes`, `estimatedTokens` and `live`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length\n- Refusal: a run with no live head prints `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}` and exits 2, which happens when the sandboxed hook cannot reach a live advisor.\n- Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. Each hook process also runs without `AI_SESSION_CHILD`, so a run from a dis\n\n### Two arms on one commit\n\n`run-command-behaviour.mjs --arm off` and `--arm on` measure one commit twice. A run under either arm starts from an environment where the advisor switch comes from the arm and not from the operator's shell, so the turn environment differs only in the advisor \n\n`--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_\n\n`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. Before it\n\nA summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` an\n\nEach arm's `startRate`, `startedOfOffered` and each policy's `started` count in `byPolicy` follow the start rule below. `startShapes` prints the lookups of the start rows as `readOnly`, so adding it back restores the `startRate` numerator of the old rule. It d\n\n### Reading the outcome\n\n- A loaded document is not a followed step, so `loadRate` and `followedRange` say a workflow opened, not that its steps were taken.\n- The behaviour rows are tuned rows, so `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` describe the corpus the brief was built against, not a fresh one.\n- Each row ran once, so one run moves a rate by more than the range around it can settle.\n- Two arms differ in more than the brief, so a `--pair` gain or loss is not the brief's effect alone.\n- The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, so the split by offer says what the Claude head carried for that row.\n\n---\n\n## 4. STRUCTURE\n\n| Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n| `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n| `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n| `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false off\n| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one sess\n| `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRun\n| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`\n| `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n| `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps th\n| `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '100,140p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | cut -c1-260\"}"},"time":{"start":1794830404537,"end":1794830405358}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794830405370,"sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","part":{"partID":"prt_1e43a8740001kyt8yxfuNwdVqT","sessionID":"ses_e1bcb8a74ffeDGpieCVUaLZQ2C","messageID":"msg_1e43a22eb0018NAF1o5GfH3pdw","type":"tool","id":"call_7280defc63ab4a94bd797381","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -n \"guarded\\|must-not-start\\|ask row\\|ask policy\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | head -20; echo '=== headings'; grep -n \"^#\\|^##\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | head -20"},"output":"135:| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `startShapes`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts as a read and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A read becomes a start only with a write inside the checkout, under the rule below. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. `startRate` counts a row as started on the same rule the decline and skill-only counts use, set out next, so a read of the command's document with no write in the checkout is a lookup and not a start. `startShapes` splits the uncapped rows that started or looked up into `skill`, `reported`, `expanded`, `readWrite` and `readOnly`, judged in that order so a run counts once under its strongest evidence. The first four sum to the started count and `readOnly` counts the lookups, and the text output prints them as a `start shapes` segment. `startedOfOffered` and each `byPolicy` `started` count follow the same rule, and `readOnly` restores only the `startRate` numerator. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n=== headings\n9:# system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n15:## 1. OVERVIEW\n17:### Purpose\n21:### Usage\n25:### Key Statistics\n35:## 2. QUICK START\n55:## 3. FEATURES\n63:### Attribution on every run line\n74:### Backfill a finished log\n87:### The typed attachment block\n96:### Measure the brief cost\n105:### Two arms on one commit\n117:### Reading the outcome\n127:## 4. STRUCTURE\n155:## 5. CONFIGURATION\n164:## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n204:## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n213:## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"135:| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `startShapes`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts as a read and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A read becomes a start only with a write inside the checkout, under the rule below. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. `startRate` counts a row as started on the same rule the decline and skill-only counts use, set out next